"""Short application notes for the added syllabus topics; deeper reasoning is optional."""
BRIEFS = {
'friction-blocks':'First assume both blocks move together. Find their common acceleration, then check whether the friction needed is within its static limit. If not, solve each block separately with kinetic friction.',
'friction-rolling':'Wheels and bearings reduce resistance by replacing sliding with rolling. Do not confuse rolling resistance with the static friction that can make a wheel start, stop, or turn without slipping.',
'gravitation-rotation':'Earth’s rotation reduces apparent gravity most at the equator and not at the poles in this spherical model. In a numerical question, use the stated latitude and rotation rate; keep gravitational field and apparent scale reading distinct.',
'gravitation-weightless':'Weightlessness means no support force, not no gravity. For a lift use its acceleration; for an orbit remember that the astronaut and spacecraft fall together.',
'kinetic-theory-specific-heats':'At constant volume, supplied heat increases internal energy. At constant pressure, some heat also supplies expansion work, so Cₚ is larger. Check whether the question uses molar heat capacity or heat capacity per kilogram.',
'laws-tension':'Equal tension throughout a string requires an ideal massless string and the appropriate frictionless pulley model. If the rope has mass, a segment must also support or accelerate the rope attached to it.',
'laws-pseudo':'Pick one frame and stay with it. In the ground frame use real forces; in a translating frame accelerating with a₀, add the pseudo force opposite a₀. Do not add it again in the ground-frame equation.',
'linear-nth':'Use this shortcut only for constant acceleration and a one-second interval. It gives signed displacement. If the object reverses direction within that second, split the journey at the turning instant before adding distances.',
'linear-variable':'First look at how acceleration is supplied: against time, position, or velocity. Choose the matching relationship in the formula card and use the initial conditions. Constant-acceleration shortcuts are not valid if a changes.',
'maths-binomial':'Rewrite the expression as a power of one plus a small fraction. Keep the first-order change only when the fraction is much smaller than one, and check whether the question needs a more accurate result.',
'maths-maxima':'A maximum or minimum is a candidate where the rate of change is zero. Check whether it is actually a peak or a trough, and also compare the allowed endpoints. A stationary point outside the physical range is not an answer.',
'oscillations-systems':'Push the object slightly away from equilibrium and find the extra force that pushes it back. If that force is proportional to displacement, use the SHM form to read off the angular frequency. Keep the model’s density and geometry assumptions.',
'plane-trajectory':'The trajectory equation lets you find height at a chosen horizontal distance without calculating time first. Complementary angles have equal range only for the same speed and equal launch and landing heights.',
'plane-incline':'For landing on an incline, choose axes along and perpendicular to the incline. Find time from the perpendicular return, then use it along the incline. The ordinary level-ground range formula does not apply.',
'rotation-equilibrium':'Balance both forces and torques. Choose a pivot through an unknown force to remove its torque from the equation. For a toppling question, compare tipping and sliding thresholds rather than assuming which happens first.',
'rotation-incline':'Identify the shape’s moment of inertia, then include both translational and rotational energy. A body with a smaller I/(mR²) accelerates faster on the same incline. Check that enough static friction exists for no-slip rolling.',
 'thermal-properties-stress':'A rod that can expand freely changes length; a rod whose expansion is prevented develops stress. For clocks and measuring scales, first work out which length changes before deciding whether the recorded value rises or falls.',
 'thermal-properties-mixing':'Before solving for a final temperature, check whether the available heat is enough to complete the phase change. If ice remains in equilibrium with water, the temperature stays at 0°C. Account for melting or condensation before using a mixing formula.',
 'thermal-properties-radiation':'Use kelvin for radiation laws. Stefan–Boltzmann gives total emitted power; Wien gives the peak wavelength. For net heat loss, subtract the radiation absorbed from the surroundings rather than using emitted power alone.',
 'thermodynamics-polytropic':'Read the given pressure–volume relation before selecting a process formula. Find the endpoint temperatures with the ideal-gas law, then calculate work and internal-energy change. Use the first law to obtain heat; it is not automatically nCᵥΔT.',
 'units-distances':'For a small parallax angle, divide the baseline by the angle in radians to estimate distance. For apparent size, multiply distance by angular size in radians. A degree value must be converted before substitution.',
 'units-conversion':'The physical quantity stays the same when its unit changes. Convert each base unit with the appropriate power: area needs a squared length conversion and density contains both mass and cubed length conversions.',
 'units-reading':'First calculate the instrument’s observed reading. Then subtract the signed zero error. A positive zero error makes readings too high; a negative one means the correction is added.',
 'vectors-unit':'Write both position vectors using the same origin. Subtract final minus initial position to obtain displacement, then use the component squares to find its magnitude. A unit vector gives direction and has magnitude one.',
 'vectors-lami':'Use Lami’s theorem only for three nonzero concurrent forces in equilibrium. Pair each force with the angle between the other two. For more forces or inconvenient angles, ordinary component equations are safer.',
 'waves-sound':'Choose the speed law for the medium: strings, solids, and gases use different stiffness and density quantities. In an ideal gas, temperature must be in kelvin. A change in the source frequency does not by itself change sound speed in the same medium.',
 'waves-intensity':'At fixed medium and frequency, intensity is proportional to amplitude squared. For a spreading point source, also account for the area over which the power spreads. Add displacements for coherent interference before squaring to find intensity.',
 'work-spring':'Measure extension from the spring’s natural length. Stored energy depends on extension squared; the work done by the spring is the negative change in that energy. For a maximum-compression question, the moving mass is instantaneously at rest.',
 'work-restitution':'Use restitution along the impact direction and momentum conservation for the isolated pair. In a bounce from a fixed floor, rebound speed is e times arrival speed and rebound height is e² times the previous height.'
}
